                <div data-article-body="true" data-track-component="article body" class="c-article-body">

                    
                    <section aria-labelledby="Abs1" data-title="Abstract" lang="en"><div class="c-article-section" id="Abs1-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Abs1">Abstract</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Abs1-content"><p>Ground-level ozone (O<sub>3</sub>) and fine particulate matter (PM<sub>2.5</sub>) remain central air quality challenges in China. Still, most evidence comes from national, regional, or megacity-scale studies, leaving medium-sized industrial cities in Northeast China poorly resolved. In Jilin City, where petrochemical, transport, and energy-related emissions interact with complex terrain and warm season meteorology, the coupled behavior of O<sub>3</sub> and PM<sub>2.5</sub> requires clearer diagnosis. This study aimed to quantify the spatiotemporal variability, meteorological sensitivity, and first-order attribution of warm season O<sub>3</sub> and PM<sub>2.5</sub> during the COVID-19 low-emission baseline and post-pandemic recovery period. We hypothesized that O<sub>3</sub> and PM<sub>2.5</sub> would respond through decoupled mechanisms—with O<sub>3</sub> showing stronger meteorological sensitivity, while PM<sub>2.5</sub> was only weakly explained by the selected meteorological predictors—a pattern that is statistically consistent with, but not directly attributable to, emission changes, given the study design. Using 3-km WRF–CMAQ simulations, surface observations, correlation analysis, multiple linear regression, and meteorological normalization, we compared May–July pollution patterns between 2020 and 2023. Model evaluation showed acceptable performance for meteorological variables and PM<sub>2.5</sub> but systematic underestimation of O<sub>3</sub>, indicating uncertainty in the absolute magnitude of simulated O<sub>3</sub>. Observed O<sub>3</sub> increased in May and June but declined slightly in July, with persistent hotspots in Panshi, whereas PM<sub>2.5</sub> increased in May, changed little in June, and declined markedly in July, while remaining relatively elevated in Huadian, Chuanying, and Jiaohe. District-level anomaly and decomposition analyses further indicated a uniform increase in May O<sub>3</sub>, heterogeneous June responses, and clearer pollutant-specific response patterns across urban and periurban districts. Meteorological predictors explained approximately 35% of O<sub>3</sub> variability, dominated by temperature, but &lt; 1% of PM<sub>2.5</sub> variability; therefore, PM<sub>2.5</sub> driver interpretation should be treated qualitatively. Normalization suggested a meteorologically associated May O<sub>3</sub> enhancement and an unexplained residual June O<sub>3</sub> enhancement, highlighting O<sub>3</sub>–PM<sub>2.5</sub> decoupling; however, because both meteorology and emissions changed simultaneously between the two simulation years, these signals reflect associations rather than controlled causal attribution.</p><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" data-test="abstract-sub-heading">Graphical abstract</h3>
<div class="c-article-section__figure" data-test="figure" data-container-section="figure"><figure><div class="c-article-section__figure-content"><div class="c-article-section__figure-item"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10661-026-15929-3/MediaObjects/10661_2026_15929_Figa_HTML.png?as=webp"><img src="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10661-026-15929-3/MediaObjects/10661_2026_15929_Figa_HTML.png" alt="" loading="lazy" width="685" height="334"></picture></div></div></figure></div></div></div></section>

                    

                    
    


                    
                        
                            
